    "## 3. The yardsticks\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two baselines to beat**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **\"Always safe\":** predict that nothing fails. It is right about 84% of the time and catches nothing. This is why we do not use accuracy.\n",
    "- **The rule of thumb:** rank shipments by `tightest_margin_hours`, the hours to spare before the cold or the clock runs out. A coordinator can do this with a calculator. If the model cannot beat it, the model is not worth having.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Four scores**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Score | Question it answers | Good value |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **ROC AUC** | Does a failing shipment rank above a safe one? | 0.50 is a coin flip, 1.00 is perfect |\n",
    "| **Average precision** | How clean is the top of the risk list? | Random is about 0.16, higher is better |\n",
    "| **Brier score** | How close are the percentages to what happens? | Lower is better. Guessing the average rate for everything scores about 0.13 |\n",
    "| **Precision and recall** | Of the alerts, how many are real? Of the failures, how many are caught? | Both high, but one trades against the other |"
   ]
  },
  {
