   "source": [
    "from sklearn.metrics import precision_recall_curve\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = go.Figure()\n",
    "STEP = 8  # plot every 8th point to keep the notebook file small\n",
    "precision, recall, _ = precision_recall_curve(y, rule_score)\n",
    "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=recall[::STEP], y=precision[::STEP], mode=\"lines\", name=\"Rule of thumb\",\n",
    "                         line=dict(color=MUTED, width=2, dash=\"dash\"),\n",
    "                         hovertemplate=\"Rule of thumb<br>Recall %{x:.0%}<br>Precision %{y:.0%}<extra></extra>\"))\n",
    "for (name, p), color in zip(proba.items(), [BLUE, ORANGE, AQUA]):\n",
    "    precision, recall, _ = precision_recall_curve(y, p)\n",
    "    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=recall[::STEP], y=precision[::STEP], mode=\"lines\", name=name, line=dict(color=color, width=2),\n",
    "                             hovertemplate=name + \"<br>Recall %{x:.0%}<br>Precision %{y:.0%}<extra></extra>\"))\n",
    "fig.add_hline(y=y.mean(), line=dict(color=GRID, width=1))\n",
    "fig.add_annotation(x=0.01, y=y.mean(), text=\"Random alerts\", showarrow=False, xanchor=\"left\", yanchor=\"bottom\",\n",
    "                   font=dict(color=MUTED))\n",
    "fig.update_xaxes(title_text=\"Recall: share of failures caught\", tickformat=\".0%\", range=[0, 1])\n",
    "fig.update_yaxes(title_text=\"Precision: share of alerts that are real\", tickformat=\".0%\", range=[0, 1.03])\n",
    "style(fig, \"Precision against recall on unseen shipments\", height=500)\n",
    "fig.update_layout(legend=dict(orientation=\"h\", y=-0.2, x=0, font=dict(color=INK)), margin=dict(b=100))\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c02aba73",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**How to read the curve:** move from left to right and the alert line is lowered, so more failures are caught (recall goes up) but more alerts are false (precision goes down). A better model has a curve that stays higher for longer.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this tells us:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **All three models beat the rule of thumb.** Average precision is about 0.72 for the models against 0.67 for the rule. The gap is roughly one and a half times the round-to-round spread, so it is real but not dramatic.\n",
    "- **The first third of failures is easy for everyone.** Up to about 35% recall, every curve sits at 100% precision. Those are the shipments whose margin has already run out, and the rule of thumb finds them as well as any model.\n",
    "- **The model earns its place in the middle of the curve.** Between about 40% and 75% recall, the models stay 10 to 15 points of precision above the rule. That is where a shipment still looks acceptable on margin alone but carries other risks, such as an unreliable carrier or a delay that is already building.\n",
    "- **The three models are close to tied.** Their dots overlap within the spread on every score."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d29c3898",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Can we trust the percentages?\n",
    "\n",
    "The app shows a number such as \"82% failure risk\" and sorts shipments into tiers. That only helps if the number is honest. We group the unseen predictions by the percentage the model gave, then check how many in each group really failed. A trustworthy model sits on the dashed line."
   ]
