   "source": [
    "model_summary = summary.drop(index=\"Rule of thumb\")\n",
    "leader = model_summary[\"avg_precision\"].idxmax()\n",
    "tie_margin = model_summary.loc[leader, \"avg_precision_spread\"]\n",
    "tied = model_summary[model_summary[\"avg_precision\"] >= model_summary.loc[leader, \"avg_precision\"] - tie_margin]\n",
    "\n",
    "CHOSEN = tied[\"brier\"].idxmin()\n",
    "chosen_proba = proba[CHOSEN]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Highest average precision      : {leader} ({model_summary.loc[leader, 'avg_precision']:.3f})\")\n",
    "print(f\"Within one spread of the leader: {list(tied.index)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Lowest Brier score among those : {CHOSEN} ({model_summary.loc[CHOSEN, 'brier']:.4f})\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Chosen model: {CHOSEN}\")\n",
    "print(\"Settings    :\", {k: v for k, v in tuned[CHOSEN].named_steps['model'].get_params().items()\n",
    "                         if k in {'C', 'n_estimators', 'max_depth', 'learning_rate', 'subsample', 'min_samples_leaf', 'max_features'}})"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f41c528",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this tells us:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- All three models are tied on average precision once the spread is taken into account, so the choice falls to the Brier score, and **gradient boosting** wins it.\n",
    "- We should be plain about how narrow this is. Random forest is almost level on every score and would also have been a reasonable choice. Gradient boosting is picked because its percentages are the most trustworthy, and the percentage is what the coordinator sees.\n",
    "- The winning settings are modest: 200 small trees, each only 2 levels deep. A small model like this has less room to memorise individual shipments."
   ]
  },
