  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b5e2cb8c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this tells us:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The tiers separate cleanly.** About 7% of Low shipments fail, 41% of Medium, 69% of High and 96% of Critical. Each step up is a clear jump, and the real rates sit inside or close to each tier's stated range.\n",
    "- **Critical means critical.** Of 264 shipments in the top tier, 252 failed. A coordinator can act on that tier without second-guessing it.\n",
    "- **The limit:** about 4 in 10 failures still sit in the Low tier. They are spread thinly across almost 3,500 shipments, each with a risk near 7%. These are failures caused by events that had not happened yet when the prediction was made, such as a sudden disruption in transit. No input available at that moment points to them, so no model could flag them without also flagging thousands of safe shipments."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "03d66cc8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Where to draw the alert line\n",
    "\n",
    "The model gives every shipment a percentage. The coordinator needs a line: above it, the shipment appears in the queue as needing action.\n",
    "\n",
    "- A **low line** catches more failures but sends more false alarms. Too many false alarms and people stop trusting the queue.\n",
    "- A **high line** means nearly every alert is real, but more failures slip through.\n",
    "\n",
    "The table shows the trade for several lines, per 1,000 shipments. The last column estimates money, using three assumptions that we state openly and that the app lets the user change:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Acting on an alert costs **$1,500** (extra dry ice, priority handling, staff time).\n",
    "- Acting in time saves the shipment **70%** of the time.\n",
    "- A failed shipment loses its full cargo value."
   ]
  },
