  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb00b968",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Decision: the alert line is 30%.**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **It matches the tiers.** 30% is where Low ends and Medium begins, so the rule for the queue is simple to say: everything Medium and above needs a look.\n",
    "- **False alarms stay low.** Per 1,000 shipments the queue holds about 126 alerts, about 3 in 4 are real, and only about 32 are false alarms. It catches about 60% of all failures.\n",
    "- **What the money column says, stated fairly.** Under our assumptions a lower line saves more, because a missed failure costs far more than a wasted intervention. A 10% line has the highest net saving in the table. But at 10% more than half the alerts are false, about 130 per 1,000 shipments instead of 32, and a coordinator who is wrong more often than right stops trusting the queue. A person's attention is the scarce resource, so we set the line for trust and let the ranking do the rest.\n",
    "- **The line is not a wall.** Shipments under 30% stay visible further down the queue, ranked by risk, cargo value and urgency. The app can expose the line as a slider so a team with more capacity can lower it."
   ]
  },
